Progress bars agents show in the chat, runner side: sys_session_progress.

Claude Code and Codex get a sys_session_progress tool through the native
relay, beside sys_session_rename. An agent calls it with a label and, when it
can tell, a percent (0-100); leaving percent out shows a bar that only says
it's busy, and clear: true ends it. It can name a log_file, the file a long
command writes its output to. The runner posts the update to the server's
POST /v1/sessions/{id}/progress (images/server/patches/
0022-agent-progress.patch), and returns what the server shows, or the
server's refusal, as the tool result.

For a log_file, omnigent/runner/progress_log.py follows the file: its last
64 KiB first, unless nothing wrote it for 30 seconds (an earlier run's log
the command is about to write again), then new lines about every second, without colour codes, with
the session's repository variable values hidden, posted to
POST /v1/sessions/{id}/progress/{bar}/log. It keeps following after the
agent's turn, so a dev server's log keeps streaming, and stops when the
server no longer keeps the bar or the file hasn't grown for 2 hours. Naming
the same file again for the same bar keeps its follower.

The tool stays out of ToolManager: upstream's tests pin the tool set every
agent registers, and it only means something in this deployment's web UI. So
the relay advertises it to a session whose agent resolved (the spec-less
fallback's set is pinned too), _ungranted_tool_reason lets it through for
every agent, and it's runner-dispatched like the rename. Codex doesn't
pre-approve it the way it does the rename; this deployment runs Codex without
approval prompts.

